    "**Discussion 1a.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Recording.* 296 segmented cells imaged at 30.08 Hz for 64 min in primary visual cortex (VISp) at 205 µm depth, i.e. layer 2/3. The Cre line (Slc17a7-IRES2-Cre) targets excitatory neurons and the indicator is GCaMP6s (Ai94 reporter). ΔF/F is the fractional change of each cell's fluorescence relative to a slowly varying baseline F0 (de Vries et al. 2020, Methods). It is an indirect, low-pass filtered read-out of spiking: GCaMP6s transients decay over roughly a second, so single spikes are not resolved in time, and the baseline normalisation removes any steady (tonic) level of activity.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Figure 1.1.* Activity depends strongly on the stimulus epoch. The population mean ΔF/F is highest during the natural movies (0.043 for `natural_movie_three`, 0.037 for `natural_movie_one`) and lower during gratings (0.020) and the grey screen (0.017). During the movies many cells are active together, which shows up as vertical bands in the image and large peaks in the population mean. During gratings a smaller set of cells responds repeatedly (horizontal streaks), as expected for orientation- or direction-selective cells. The mouse is mostly stationary: running comes in brief bouts of a few seconds, and some of them coincide with population events. The correlation between the 1-s smoothed population mean and running speed is only r = 0.28, so running modulates activity but does not explain most of it.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Figure 1.2.* Responses to the repeated movie differ widely between cells. The most reliable cell (220, inter-repeat r = 0.81) produces transients at the same movie times (about 0.5 s and 8 s) on all 10 repeats, but their amplitude and the presence of smaller events (11–13 s) still vary between repeats. A median cell (131, r = 0.12) responds near 17 s on only some repeats, and cell 108 (r = 0.06) shows no consistent response. The reliability histogram shows that reliable, stimulus-locked cells are a minority: the median r is 0.12 and only 38 of 296 cells have r > 0.3. As a cross-check we also computed the \"oracle score\" used by the MICrONS Consortium (2025), the correlation of each repeat with the mean of the other nine. It ranks cells almost identically (Spearman ρ = 0.97) and gives the same top five cells, so the choice of reliability measure does not affect which cells count as reliable.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Limitations of ΔF/F to keep in mind:* the slow decay blurs spike timing, the relation between spikes and fluorescence is non-linear (it saturates for bursts), the noise s.d. (≈ 0.045 ΔF/F) hides isolated single spikes, and some neuropil contamination remains after subtraction."
   ]
