    "## 1b. Behavioural state as a Markov chain"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9542f866",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Method.** Running speed is median-filtered over 1 s (this removes encoder jitter, which gives speeds of ±1–2 cm/s even when the mouse is still) and thresholded to give a two-state sequence $s_n \\in \\{0 = \\text{stationary}, 1 = \\text{running}\\}$ at the imaging frame rate.\n",
    "\n",
    "Following the lecture convention (Lecture 3), the transition matrix is *left stochastic*: $T_{ji} = P(s_{n+1}=j \\mid s_n=i)$, so its columns sum to 1 and a probability vector evolves as $\\mathbf p(n+1) = T\\,\\mathbf p(n)$. The maximum-likelihood estimate is the number of $i \\to j$ transitions divided by the number of frames spent in $i$. The stationary distribution $\\boldsymbol\\pi$ satisfies $T\\boldsymbol\\pi = \\boldsymbol\\pi$, i.e. it is the eigenvector of $T$ with eigenvalue 1, normalised to sum to 1 (as in Exercise Sheet 3, Q1d).\n",
    "\n",
    "For a Markov chain the time spent in state $i$ before leaving is **geometric**: $P(L = k) = T_{ii}^{\\,k-1}(1-T_{ii})$, so the survival function $P(L \\ge k) = T_{ii}^{\\,k-1}$ is a straight line on a log y-axis, the mean is $1/(1-T_{ii})$ frames and the CV is $\\sqrt{T_{ii}} \\approx 1$. Equivalently, the **hazard** (the probability of leaving in the next frame, given the time already spent in the state) is the constant $1-T_{ii}$: the chain has no memory. The plots below test exactly this."
   ]
  },
