    "**Discussion 1b.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Threshold.* In the speed histogram the stationary frames form a narrow peak between about −1 and +0.5 cm/s (encoder noise: the raw trace even goes negative), and running forms a broad shoulder from about 2 to 10 cm/s. 2 cm/s therefore sits just above the noise peak. The sensitivity table shows that the conclusions do not depend on the exact value: between 1 and 3 cm/s the running fraction changes from 0.144 to 0.102 and the transition probabilities by less than 30%. Only at 5 cm/s are running bouts visibly cut short (mean 1.3 s).\n",
    "\n",
    "*Transition matrix and stationary distribution.* $P(\\text{stat}\\to\\text{run}) = 0.0016$ and $P(\\text{run}\\to\\text{stat}) = 0.0117$ per frame. The eigenvalues of $T$ are 1 and $\\lambda_2 = 1 - 0.0016 - 0.0117 = 0.9867$. The eigenvector for eigenvalue 1 gives $\\pi_\\text{run} = 0.121$, identical to the observed fraction of time running (0.121), and $\\lambda_2$ gives a relaxation time to $\\boldsymbol\\pi$ of only 2.5 s. **This agreement is not evidence that the mouse is Markovian.** For a two-state chain estimated by counting, $\\pi_\\text{run} = p/(p+q)$ reproduces the observed occupancy automatically, because the numbers of stationary→running and running→stationary transitions differ by at most one. The same is true of the mean bout lengths. A real test needs the shape of the dwell-time distributions.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Dwell times.* **Running bouts** are consistent with a Markov chain: the real and simulated survival curves overlap (KS p = 0.12), the CV is 1.19 against 1.01 simulated, and the hazard is roughly flat around $1-T_{11}$. **Stationary bouts are not.** They have the same mean (20.6 s real against 21.4 s simulated, which is fixed by $T$), but the median is 1.15 s against 15 s, the CV is 2.35 against 1.00, and KS p = 10⁻²⁶. The real survival curve drops steeply at first (many sub-2-s \"pauses\" inside running episodes) and then has a long tail (rests of up to 275 s). The hazard of starting to run *decreases* with time already spent still, from about 10⁻² per frame in the first second to about 4 × 10⁻⁴ after 10 s. The longer the mouse has rested, the less likely it is to start running, which is a form of memory that a first-order Markov chain cannot have.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Interpretation and caveats.* The stationary state is really a mixture of two processes, brief pauses and genuine rests, each with its own time scale. A three-state hidden Markov model (running / pause / rest) or a semi-Markov model with non-geometric dwell times would capture this. Caveats: each state has only about 160 bouts, the censored first and last bouts are discarded, and short \"pauses\" partly depend on the smoothing and threshold."
