    "**Method.** Both surrogates are simulated on a 1-ms grid over exactly the 300 s spanned by the 10 movie repeats, and the spikes are then binned onto the real imaging frames. $N = 296$ model neurons are simulated, one per real cell.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Baseline firing rates* are drawn from a log-normal distribution with median 1 Hz and $\\sigma_{\\ln r} = 1$, so 95% of rates lie between about 0.14 and 7 Hz (clipped to 0.05–40 Hz). Firing rates across cortical neurons are strongly skewed and approximately log-normal, with most pyramidal cells firing at low rates (Buzsáki & Mizuseki 2014). We tried to estimate rates from the data by counting calcium events, but the counts depended mostly on the detection threshold, so the rate scale here is an assumption. Note that ΔF/F only constrains the product of rate and amplitude A (1d), and the spread of the distribution is checked against the real cell-to-cell spread of transient sizes in 1d.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Surrogate A (null), a renewal process.* Each inter-spike interval is an absolute refractory period $t_\\text{ref} = 2$ ms plus an exponential waiting time with rate $\\lambda' = 1/(1/r - t_\\text{ref})$, so that the mean rate is $r$. This is the exponential-waiting-time method of Exercise Sheet 3, with the refractory period from Lecture 3. Theory: the ISI density is $\\lambda' e^{-\\lambda'(\\tau - t_\\text{ref})}$ for $\\tau > t_\\text{ref}$, $CV_{ISI} = 1 - r\\,t_\\text{ref}$ (just below 1), and the Fano factor tends to $CV^2$ for long counting windows. There is no dependence on stimulus or behaviour.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Surrogate B (driven), an inhomogeneous Poisson process* with\n",
    "$$\\lambda_i(t) = r_i \\, f_i(t_\\text{movie}) \\, g(s(t)), \\qquad f_i = e^{\\beta z_i}/\\langle e^{\\beta z_i}\\rangle,$$\n",
    "where $z_i$ is Gaussian white noise smoothed with a 150-ms Gaussian kernel and normalised to unit variance. $f_i$ is a smooth, positive, random function of time within the movie with mean 1, and it is **identical on every repeat** (stimulus locking). $\\beta = 1.5$ makes it peaky, a few strong \"events\" per repeat, like the reliable real cells. The gain is $g = 1$ when stationary and $g = 2$ when running, using the state sequence **simulated** in 1b. The factor of about two follows the locomotion-related increase in mouse V1 visual responses reported by Niell & Stryker (2010). Spike counts per 1-ms bin are Poisson with mean $\\lambda\\,\\Delta t$, as in `mouse_sim.sim_Poisson_nrns` from the lectures. For a known $\\lambda(t)$ the ISI density is\n",
    "$$p(\\tau) = \\frac{\\int \\lambda(t)\\,\\lambda(t+\\tau)\\,e^{-\\int_t^{t+\\tau}\\lambda(u)\\,du}\\,dt}{\\int \\lambda(t)\\,dt},$$\n",
    "which is evaluated numerically below and compared with a homogeneous Poisson process of the same mean rate.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Spike-train statistics (Lecture 3, Exercise Sheet 3, `demo_w4_spiketrains.py`):*\n",
    "* $CV_{ISI}$, the s.d. over the mean of all ISIs;\n",
    "* the **CV₂** of Holt et al. (1996), $CV_2 = \\langle 2|\\Delta t_{i+1}-\\Delta t_i|/(\\Delta t_{i+1}+\\Delta t_i)\\rangle$. It compares only *adjacent* intervals, so it is close to 1 for a Poisson process but almost insensitive to rate changes that are slow compared with the ISI. It separates spiking irregularity from rate modulation, which CV cannot do;\n",
    "* the correlation between successive ISIs, which is 0 for a renewal process (i.i.d. intervals; the lecture's test of the renewal assumption);\n",
    "* the Fano factor of spike counts."
