    "**Discussion 1c.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Surrogate A.* The raster of the example neuron (baseline 5 Hz) shows no alignment across repeats, as intended. Its ISI histogram follows the shifted exponential (a straight line on the log axis) with CV = 0.99, matching the theory $1 - r\\,t_\\text{ref} = 0.99$. The shortest ISI over all neurons is 2.003 ms, i.e. the refractory period is respected. Across neurons the median CV is 0.98, and the Fano factor of counts in 1-s windows is 0.99, close to 1 as for a Poisson process. Renewal theory gives Fano → CV² (≈ 0.95) only for windows much longer than the typical ISI, and at about 1.5 Hz a 1-s window holds only one or two spikes. Across the 10 repeats the Fano factor is 0.98. With only 10 repeats it matters that the variance is computed with $n-1$ in the denominator: the biased estimator gives about 0.88, i.e. 9/10 of the true value.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Surrogate B.* Spikes line up vertically across repeats, following the rate profile (grey), so the response is stimulus-locked. The ISI distribution is clearly **not** exponential. It has an excess of short ISIs (spikes within high-rate events) and a long tail (gaps between events), giving CV = 1.99 for the example neuron (median 1.52 across neurons). The numerically computed inhomogeneous-Poisson density follows the histogram, whereas the homogeneous Poisson curve with the same mean rate does not. The two Fano factors make the same point. Across repeats, at a fixed movie time, the count variability is Poisson (Fano ≈ 1.0, slightly above 1 because the running gain differs between repeats). Along time it is 2.7, because windows with different rates are pooled. A Fano factor or CV computed over time on a non-stationary process measures rate modulation as well as spiking variability, which is the point made in Exercise Sheet 3, Q5.\n",
    "\n",
    "The local measures confirm this. For A, CV₂ = 0.99 and the serial ISI correlation is −0.002, as expected for a renewal process with i.i.d. intervals. For B, CV₂ = 1.16 is much closer to the Poisson value of 1 than CV (1.52), because comparing only adjacent intervals removes most of the rate modulation. It does not fall all the way to 1 because B's rate changes on a 150-ms scale, faster than the typical ISI (about 0.6 s at 1.7 Hz), and Holt et al. note that CV₂ only removes changes that are slow compared with the ISI. B's successive ISIs are positively correlated (median 0.10): short intervals cluster inside high-rate events. So B is not a renewal process, even though it is Poisson at every instant.\n",
    "\n",
    "*What is unrealistic.* A has no stimulus, no behaviour and no correlations. In B every neuron is strongly and reliably driven (in the real data only about 13% of cells are), the profiles of different neurons are independent (no shared fluctuations), the gain is the same for every neuron, there is no refractoriness or bursting, and the trial-to-trial variability is exactly Poisson, whereas cortical responses are usually more variable than that."
   ]
