   "source": [
    "**Method.** The calcium concentration follows the AR(1) model from Lecture 4, driven by the spike count $s(t)$ in each frame: $c(t) = a\\,c(t-1) + A\\,s(t)$, with $a = e^{-\\Delta t/\\tau}$ and $\\Delta t = 1/f_s$. This is equivalent to convolving the spike train with an exponential kernel, as in `mouse_sim.gen_calcium_transients`.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Decay constant $\\tau$ = 0.864 s.** This is the GCaMP6s value used in the course model (Week 2 slides, \"time constant 0.864 sec, corresponding to GCaMP6s\", and `mouse_sim.py`). We checked it against our own data: the average of 14,201 large, isolated transients decays with a fitted $\\tau$ = 0.85 s. This gives $a$ = 0.9623 at 30.08 Hz. *(If you cite a primary source such as Chen et al. 2013, check the exact value there and convert a half-decay time with $\\tau = t_{1/2}/\\ln 2$.)*\n",
    "* **Noise.** Gaussian white noise with s.d. equal to the median real-cell noise. The noise is estimated from first differences, $\\sigma = 1.4826\\,\\mathrm{MAD}(\\Delta x)/\\sqrt 2$: differencing removes the slow calcium signal and the MAD ignores the transients.\n",
    "* **Baseline.** Real ΔF/F is measured relative to a baseline F0 that absorbs any steady level of activity. The raw AR model has no such baseline: a neuron firing at $r$ Hz settles at $A r \\Delta t/(1-a) > 0$. We therefore subtract each model cell's median.\n",
    "* **Amplitude A.** A single value for both surrogates (same indicator), chosen so that the median over cells of (99th percentile − median) matches the real data."
   ]
