    "**Discussion 1e.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Autocorrelation.* At short lags all three datasets decay almost exactly as $e^{-\\text{lag}/0.864\\,\\text{s}}$ (ACF at 1 s: 0.27 real, 0.26 A, 0.32 B). On this time scale the ACF is set by the **indicator kernel**, not by neural dynamics, so it cannot distinguish the models. At long lags the real data have a peak at 30 s (mean ACF 0.12) and again at 60 s, the stimulus-locked component. A has none. B has a much larger one (0.45), because every model neuron is driven identically on every repeat. The median repeat reliability tells the same story: 0.12 real, 0.55 B, ≈ 0 A.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Distribution of ΔF/F.* The real values are sharply peaked at 0 with a heavy tail (median skewness 4.3). The cells are silent most of the time, punctuated by large transients. A is close to Gaussian (skewness 0.85): steady firing at about 1.5 Hz with a 0.86-s decay sums many spikes, so the central limit theorem applies. B has a heavier tail (skewness 2.4) because its spikes come in rate events, but it is still less sparse than the real data. Both surrogates also have negative values down to −0.3 that the real data lack, an artefact of the median baseline subtraction.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Correlations between cells.* The mean pairwise correlation is 0.073 for the real data, 0.003 for A and 0.038 for B. B's correlation comes only from the shared running gain (its stimulus profiles are independent between neurons).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Captured.** A captures the indicator time scale, noise level and amplitude, which is everything that does not depend on stimulus or behaviour. B adds stimulus locking at the 30-s period and modulation by running. **Missing from both:** sparse, bursty single-cell activity; heterogeneity of stimulus drive (most real cells are weakly or unreliably driven, while B drives all of them strongly); and shared population fluctuations that are neither stimulus nor running (see the real PC1 in Q2 and Q4). In B the running gain is also applied at the *simulated* running times, not the real ones."
   ]
