    "## 2a. Choosing the embedding delay τ"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dba64896",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Method.** If τ is much shorter than the internal time scales, successive delay coordinates are almost identical and the reconstruction collapses onto the diagonal. If τ is much longer, the coordinates are independent and the points fill a structureless cloud (Lecture 4 and the 2-D example below). Two criteria from the lectures:\n",
    "\n",
    "* the **first zero of the autocorrelation function** (in practice \"go back a bit\", because by then the coordinates are already nearly independent);\n",
    "* the **first minimum of the auto mutual information** $I(x_t; x_{t+\\tau})$ (Fraser & Swinney 1986). This is the criterion we use, because it measures any statistical dependence, not just linear correlation, which matters for these sparse, strongly non-Gaussian signals. If there is no minimum we fall back on the first zero of the ACF.\n",
    "\n",
    "The AMI is computed with the plug-in estimator (Lecture 9) using 16 equiprobable (\"uniform count\") bins, the binning recommended by Timme & Lapish (2018) because every bin is well populated and outliers cannot dominate. The plug-in estimate is biased upwards by finite sampling. The Panzeri–Treves expression (Pola et al., Lecture 9) gives a bias of about $(B-1)^2/(2N\\ln 2) \\approx 0.018$ bits for $B = 16$ bins and $N = 9{,}030$ points. This is drawn on the AMI plot, and because it is the same at all lags it does not move the position of the minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "Bradley & Kantz (2015) add two practical points. One wants *the smallest reasonable* τ, because a large τ lets the reconstruction fold over on itself. And the embedding theorems require τ not to be a multiple of an orbit's period, here the 30-s movie, which all our delays (≤ 3 s) satisfy."
   ]
  },
