    "**Discussion 2a.** The chosen delays are 2.03 s for the real neuron, 0.80 s for the real PC1, 1.30 s / 2.29 s for A and 2.53 s / 2.69 s for B (neuron / PC1). In every case the AMI minimum lies between the 1/e decay time of the ACF and its first zero, which is the range the lecture recommends. The AMI at the chosen delay (0.05–0.23 bits) is still 3–13 times the plug-in bias (0.018 bits), so these minima are real features of the signals, not estimation noise. The delays are of the order of 1–3 calcium decay constants, so they are set largely by the indicator and the smoothness of the drive rather than by intrinsic neural dynamics. The real PC1 has the shortest delay because its ACF drops sharply in the first frame: the uncorrelated measurement noise in this weak signal reduces the ACF at every non-zero lag.\n",
    "\n",
    "The 2-D plots of the real neuron reproduce the lecture's calcium example. At τ = 1 frame the points lie along the diagonal (redundant coordinates). At τ = 2 s they spread into an L shape: most points sit in the baseline corner, and transients appear as excursions out along one axis and back along the other. At τ = 5 s the picture is similar, because for such a sparse signal most of the \"structure\" is the baseline. Changing τ by a factor of two in either direction mainly changes how open the excursions are."
