   "source": [
    "**Method (Kennel et al. 1992).** For each delay vector in $m$ dimensions, find its nearest neighbour, excluding points less than 0.5 s apart in time (a Theiler window, so that neighbours on the same transient do not count). Then add the $(m+1)$-th coordinate. The neighbour is *false* if the distance grows too much, $|x_{i+m\\tau} - x_{j+m\\tau}|/R_m > R_\\text{tol} = 15$, or if the new distance is large compared with the spread of the signal, $R_{m+1}/\\sigma > A_\\text{tol} = 2$. For a deterministic system the fraction of false neighbours falls to about zero at the minimum embedding dimension (the Hénon map at m = 2 and the Lorenz system at m = 3 in the lecture examples). Neighbour searches use a k-d tree. The dashed curve repeats the analysis on surrogate B **without measurement noise**, to separate the effect of measurement noise from that of the stochastic spiking.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Surrogate-data test (Theiler et al. 1992; Bradley & Kantz 2015).** Surrogates A and B test specific *models*. The standard test for determinism asks a different question: could a *linear* stochastic process with the same autocorrelation and the same amplitude distribution give the same result? We generate 19 IAAFT surrogates of each real signal (Schreiber & Schmitz 1996). The Fourier phases are randomised while the power spectrum is kept, and the values are then rank-remapped onto the original amplitude distribution, iterating until both are matched. This is the \"surrogate data\" idea of the Week 2 lecture: keep the low-order statistics and randomise everything else. If the real false-neighbour fraction lies below all 19 surrogates at some m, the linear null hypothesis is rejected at p = 0.05 (one-sided rank test). The phase-randomised surrogates also keep the 30-s periodicity, because it is part of the power spectrum.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Due diligence (Bradley & Kantz 2015).** The analysis is repeated on the first and last five repeats separately, to check whether the result is stable or changes with time (non-stationarity)."
   ]
