    "**Discussion 2b.** **No signal reaches a false-neighbour fraction of zero, so there is no embedding dimension at which the data behave like a low-dimensional deterministic system.**\n",
    "\n",
    "* All curves fall from about 99% at m = 1 to about 15% at m = 4. The noise surrogate A falls just as fast, so this initial drop is **not** evidence of determinism. It comes from the smoothness the calcium kernel imposes: in a few delay dimensions, neighbouring points share the same local, slowly decaying piece of trajectory.\n",
    "* Above m ≈ 5 the false fraction of A **rises** (33% at m = 10). This is the typical signature of a stochastic signal: as m grows, nearest neighbours become further apart and the $A_\\text{tol}$ criterion flags them. The real PC1 behaves the same way (36% at m = 10), again suggesting that the population signal is dominated by noise-like fluctuations. The real neuron levels off at about 10%, between A (rising) and B (about 6%).\n",
    "* Removing the measurement noise from B changes almost nothing. The false neighbours left in B therefore come from Poisson spiking and the running gain, the model's own stochasticity, not from measurement noise. That the real neuron sits above B implies more trial-to-trial variability than a Poisson model with identical drive on every repeat.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Surrogate-data test.** For the **real neuron**, the false-neighbour curve lies inside the range of the 19 IAAFT surrogates for all m ≥ 5, and only marginally below it at m = 3–4 (33.6% against a surrogate minimum of 34.0%, and 17.7% against 19.0%). Its embedding is therefore essentially indistinguishable from a linear Gaussian process with the same spectrum and amplitude distribution, and there is no convincing evidence of non-linear determinism. For the **real PC1**, the false-neighbour fraction is below all 19 surrogates for every m from 5 to 10. That rejects the linear null hypothesis, so the population signal contains structure that linear correlations alone cannot produce. Two caveats temper this. Testing many values of m is a multiple comparison (although neighbouring m are strongly correlated). More importantly, Bradley & Kantz warn about the **non-stationarity trap**: surrogates are stationary by construction, so a difference from them can reflect non-stationarity rather than non-linearity. The halves test shows exactly this kind of non-stationarity for PC1, whose false-neighbour fraction at m = 10 is 39% in repeats 1–5 and 54% in repeats 6–10, whereas the neuron's curve is stable (13% and 10%). Since PC1 tracks the population gain and running (2, 4b), the most likely source is the changing behavioural state, not low-dimensional deterministic dynamics.\n",
    "\n",
    "This matches Bradley & Kantz's description of noise-driven systems: they produce an invariant measure that fills the whole embedding space without fractal structure, so no embedding dimension removes all false neighbours, and since every noisy point affects m delay vectors, \"bigger m is not better\".\n",
    "\n",
    "Caveats: the result depends on $R_\\text{tol}$, $A_\\text{tol}$ and the Theiler window, on the limited data length (9,030 points), and on the fact that at m = 10 with τ ≈ 2 s each delay vector spans 18 s, more than half a movie repeat. Bradley & Kantz point out that the window m·τ is what really matters."
