    "**Discussion 2c.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*What the reconstructions show.* The real neuron's reconstruction is a dense core at baseline with excursions (\"arms\") that are consistently coloured: early-movie transients (purple) and late-movie transients (yellow) trace the same paths on different repeats. B looks similar but cleaner. A is an isotropic blob with no colour organisation. For the population signal, the real PC1 is a cloud with only weak colour ordering, whereas B's PC1 traces loops coloured by movie time. We quantified this by asking whether each point's nearest neighbour in the 3-D reconstruction (outside the Theiler window) belongs to the same movie moment (±0.5 s) on another repeat. The answer is yes for 21% of points for the real neuron, about six times the chance level of 3.3% and the same as B (21%), while A is at chance (3.8%). For the real PC1 it is only 7.8%, against 39% for B.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Low-dimensional deterministic dynamics?* No. The false-neighbour fraction never reaches zero, the best neuron's embedding is indistinguishable from linear surrogates with the same spectrum, the only departure (PC1) coincides with non-stationarity, and DET in Q3 does not robustly separate the real data from noise. The structure that does exist is **recurrence locked to the stimulus**: the same input produces a similar excursion. It is not the closed, thin trajectory of a low-dimensional attractor.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Noise and the slow indicator.* The calcium indicator acts as a low-pass filter with its own linear dynamics (the AR(1) model). It makes even white-noise spiking look like a smooth trajectory, which is why the early FNN drop and the short-lag ACF are the same for A. The reconstructed \"dynamics\" therefore partly belong to the indicator, not the neurons. Measurement noise inflates the false-neighbour fraction at high m and blurs the trajectories, although for B the stochastic spiking matters more than measurement noise.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Takens' theorem.* The theorem guarantees that delay vectors reconstruct the attractor of an **autonomous** deterministic system observed through a smooth measurement. Visual cortex watching a movie is a **driven** system, $\\mathbf x_{t+1} = F(\\mathbf x_t, \\mathbf u_t)$, where the input $\\mathbf u_t$ (the movie) is itself periodic with a 30-s period, and it also receives running-related and internal fluctuations that are neither deterministic nor stationary. At best a delay embedding reconstructs the combined system of cortex plus stimulus, and recurrences at 30 s reflect the period of the forcing rather than intrinsic dynamics. Our reconstructions should therefore be read as *descriptive* pictures of stimulus-locked trajectories plus noise, not as reconstructions of an attractor. Bradley & Kantz make the same point: embedding data from a system whose parameters change, here the input and the behavioural state, produces a mixture of different dynamics rather than one attractor. A more principled analysis would treat the stimulus as an explicit input (an input-output model), or look at the grey-screen epoch, which is closer to autonomous but still non-stationary because of running."
   ]
