    "**Method.**\n",
    "* **State vector.** Each cell is z-scored, activity is averaged into 0.1-s bins (3 frames, giving 3,010 points and a 3,010 × 3,010 matrix), and the binned activity is projected onto the first `K_PC = 20` principal components. Using PCs rather than all 296 cells removes the dimensions with the least variance (mostly noise) and keeps the distance computation cheap, while Euclidean distances in the retained subspace still approximate those between full z-scored population vectors. The 20 PCs capture 43% of the real variance (22% A, 54% B). There is no definitive rule for how many components to keep (Gillies, PCA notes). Rather than rely on one value, 3c shows how the conclusions change for K = 5–50.\n",
    "* **Distance:** Euclidean, $R_{ij} = \\Theta(\\varepsilon - \\lVert\\mathbf x_i - \\mathbf x_j\\rVert)$ (Lecture 4).\n",
    "* **Tolerance ε:** chosen for each dataset so that the **recurrence rate is fixed at 5%** (the 5th percentile of all pairwise distances). A fixed ε would not be comparable across datasets: A's high-dimensional noise has much larger typical distances (ε = 8.3 against 5.5 for the real data) and would show almost no recurrences. Fixing RR makes the *structure* of the plots comparable.\n",
    "* **RQA:** the recurrence rate RR and the determinism DET (the fraction of recurrent points lying on diagonal lines at least `LMIN` bins long) are computed over the upper triangle, excluding diagonals within a Theiler window of 3 s. After about 3.5 calcium decay constants the indicator's own autocorrelation is below 0.05, so trivial recurrences from the slow decay are excluded. This is Theiler's (1986) correction, which makes sure the pairs counted are effectively independent samples.\n",
    "* **Non-stationarity:** recurrence plots are one of the few techniques that remain informative for non-stationary data (Bradley & Kantz 2015). Here they show directly *when* the population state changes, e.g. with running."
   ]
