    "**Discussion 3a.** *Effect of ε:* at RR = 1% only the closest pairs recur and the plot is sparse. At 5–10% faint diagonal stripes at 30-s offsets become visible. At 20% the plot is dominated by large blocks of \"quiet\" states that are close to each other simply because little is happening. Small ε gives noisy, sparse plots, and large ε fills in everything and hides the fine structure.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Real data:* the plot has a block or checkerboard structure. Recurrent bands correspond to stationary periods resembling one another, and white bands (about 0–25 s, 120–150 s and 180–200 s) coincide with repeats 1, 5 and 7, when the mouse ran. During running the population state moves far from all other states. Inside the stationary blocks there are faint lines parallel to the main diagonal at multiples of 30 s. *Surrogate A:* speckle with vertical and horizontal stripes (moments of low overall activity, close to everything) and a thick main diagonal produced by the slow calcium decay. *Surrogate B:* clean diagonal lines at offsets of 30, 60, … s, interrupted where the simulated running gain pushes the state away."
