   "source": [
    "**Discussion 3c.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Lines parallel to the main diagonal* occur when two stretches of the trajectory run alongside each other in state space, i.e. the same *sequence* of population states is repeated. Here the repeated movie produces them, so they occur at lags equal to multiples of the 30-s movie length. The recurrence rate as a function of lag makes this quantitative. The real data have peaks at every multiple of 30 s, with RR = 0.17 at 30 s against 0.044 at 15 s (about four times the background). B has much higher peaks (0.78 at 30 s, essentially 0 between). A and the spontaneous epoch show no peaks (0.047 and 0.052 at 30 s and 15 s for spontaneous activity).\n",
    "\n",
    "*RQA.* RR is about 5% for all datasets by construction. DET (LMIN = 2 bins) is 0.82 for the real data, 0.81 for A, 0.99 for B and 0.63 for spontaneous activity. With longer minimum lines (10 bins = 1 s) it is 0.14, 0.13, 0.87 and 0.01. **DET does not separate the real data from the noise surrogate A.** Because the calcium kernel is slow, any chance recurrence lasts several bins and so forms a short diagonal line, which means DET measures the smoothness of the signal as much as determinism. B's long lines reflect the deterministic repetition of its input. DET is higher during the movie than during the grey screen, which is consistent with the movie imposing repeatable sequences of states.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Robustness (sensitivity tables).* Following Bradley & Kantz's advice to repeat the analysis while varying the parameters, we varied the number of PCs (5–50) and the recurrence rate (2–10%). **The 30-s recurrence peak of the real data is robust.** RR at 30 s is 2.7–5.5 times RR at 15 s for every setting, while A (0.9–1.6) and spontaneous activity (0.85–1.1) stay near 1 and B is far higher. **The DET comparison is not robust.** With 5 PCs the real data have *lower* DET than A (0.17 against 0.39 for LMIN = 5), at 20 PCs they are equal (0.41 against 0.44), and at 50 PCs the real data are higher (0.54 against 0.36). Spontaneous activity always has the lowest DET. We therefore rely on the lag-resolved recurrence rate, not on DET, for conclusions about reliability.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Reliability, spontaneous activity and surrogates.* The real population response to the movie is **partially** reliable: there are clear 30-s recurrences that spontaneous activity and A lack, but they are far weaker than the purely stimulus-driven, Poisson-variable model B produces. The real cortex therefore has much more trial-to-trial variability, much of it shared across cells, than B.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Running.* At the same movie time, two repeats recur 25% of the time when the mouse is stationary in both, but only 4% when it runs in either (a factor of about six). Each repeat's reliability (its mean recurrence with the others) falls with the fraction of the repeat spent running (Spearman ρ = −0.68). Repeats 1 and 5, with 54% and 81% running, are the least reliable. B shows the same effect by construction (gain ×2). Caveats: with Euclidean distance a pure gain change appears as non-recurrence even if the *pattern* of active cells is unchanged (a cosine distance would be gain-invariant); there are only 10 repeats; and running is confounded with which repeat it is."
   ]
