   "source": [
    "**Method.** Each grating is shown for 2 s, followed by 1 s of grey. The **response** of each cell is its mean ΔF/F over the whole 2-s presentation minus the mean over the last 0.5 s of the preceding grey interval (the baseline). The full 2 s is used because GCaMP6s rises over a few hundred milliseconds and decays with τ ≈ 0.86 s, so the calcium signal integrates over the whole presentation. A later window would add little, and extending it past 2 s would mix in the next stimulus. Blank presentations are excluded, leaving 598 presentations × 296 cells. PCA (sklearn, equivalent to an eigendecomposition of the data covariance matrix, Lecture 2) is applied in two versions:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Covariance PCA** of the mean-centred ΔF/F responses, the main analysis (`STANDARDISE_G = False`). All cells are measured in the same units, and Lever et al. (2017) advise that when variables are already on the same scale, standardising \"is not normally appropriate, as it may actually distort the data\": a cell whose response varies strongly with the stimulus would be given the same weight as one that varies only through noise.\n",
    "* **PCA of z-scored cells** (equivalent to a correlation-matrix PCA), in which every cell contributes equally regardless of its response amplitude. In calcium imaging ΔF/F amplitudes also depend on each cell's indicator expression, so this is a reasonable alternative. PCA is not scale invariant, and Lever et al. ask that when different scalings are tried, both are described, so both are reported below.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "To interpret each PC we report η² = SS_between / SS_total of the PC scores grouped by direction, temporal frequency (TF), running state and grating block, i.e. the fraction of that PC's variance explained by each variable."
   ]
