    "**Discussion 4a.**\n",
    "\n",
    "**The answer depends strongly on how the cells are scaled**, which is the point of Lever et al. (2017).\n",
    "\n",
    "*Covariance PCA (main analysis, figures above).* PC1–PC3 explain 35%, 20% and 12% of the variance, so 50% is reached with only 2 components. All of the top four PCs are organised by **grating direction** (η² = 0.45, 0.51, 0.30, 0.22). TF explains at most 7%, and running and block almost nothing. In 3-D the presentations form a star: a dense centre with arms, each arm belonging mainly to one direction (for example 0° along +PC1). The loadings explain why. 98% of PC1's squared loading sits in just 10 cells, so the covariance PCs are essentially the responses of a handful of strongly responsive, sharply direction-tuned cells, and each arm is one of those cells responding to its preferred direction.\n",
    "\n",
    "*PCA of z-scored cells.* PC1 explains 29% of the variance and each later component about 3% or less, so 50% needs 19 components: one dominant dimension plus a high-dimensional tail. Here **PC1 is a gain axis, not a stimulus axis.** 99% of cells load positively on it, and its weight is spread over the population (the top 10 cells hold only 8% of the squared loading). Its scores correlate with the mean response across cells (ρ = 0.96) and *negatively* with the population ΔF/F in the baseline window (ρ = −0.84). Direction, TF, running and block each explain at most 9% of its variance. PC1 therefore mainly measures how high the population still was before the grating appeared. The same axis exists in the covariance PCA, but only as PC5 (3.4% of the variance; ρ = +0.76 with the baseline). It ranks first only once z-scoring gives the many weakly responsive cells the same weight as the few strong ones. The grey interval lasts only 1 s, and the baseline window covers 0.5–1 s after the previous grating ended, so between about 30% and 55% of the previous response ($e^{-1/0.864}$ to $e^{-0.5/0.864}$) is still present in the baseline and is then subtracted. **In the z-scored analysis our choice of baseline window therefore creates the largest source of variance**, which is a limitation. Alternatives would be a longer gap (not available here), regressing out the baseline, or not subtracting it. The stimulus structure of the z-scored PCA is in PC2–PC5, where direction explains 52%, 42%, 42% and 30% of the variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Interpretation.* Both scalings agree that **direction (orientation) is the dominant stimulus variable**, that temporal frequency is weakly represented, and that running (only 14% of presentations) does not form its own axis. Running trials are scattered throughout. They disagree on what dominates the variance: a few strongly tuned cells (covariance) or a shared gain and baseline fluctuation across many weakly responsive cells (z-scored). Direction is encoded by distinct, sparse sets of cells, so a linear, variance-maximising method captures it in few dimensions only when those cells dominate the variance. The question also matters: covariance PCA is better for finding the stimulus axes, while the z-scored version exposes the population-wide gain, which is itself a real feature of the data (see 4b and Q2)."
   ]
