    "**Discussion 4b.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Trajectories.* For B the trajectory traces repeatable loops ordered by movie time, and the average over repeats (black) follows them. A is an unstructured cloud. The real trajectory lies between the two: points are partly ordered by colour, but the repeat average is small compared with the scatter, so single-trial variability dominates the top PCs. The PC1 time course correlates between repeats at 0.38 for the real data, 0.59 for B and 0.04 for A.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Dimensionality.* PC1 explains 11.1% (real), 1.3% (A) and 5.5% (B) of the variance. 50% of the variance needs 29, 58 and 17 PCs, and the participation ratio $(\\sum\\lambda)^2/\\sum\\lambda^2$ is 48, 164 and 51. A is close to the high-dimensional, nearly isotropic spectrum expected of independent neurons (a perfectly flat spectrum would give 296, and finite sampling lowers it). The real data are **much lower-dimensional than independent activity**, about as low as B, but for a different reason. B's low dimensionality comes from the smooth, stimulus-locked rate profiles (the participation ratio is set by how many independent \"events\" the 30-s profiles contain). The real data's comes mainly from shared fluctuations: PC1 is the population mean, only partly locked to the movie and partly linked to running.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Scaling check.* Repeating the PCA on unscaled ΔF/F gives participation ratios of 22 (real), 88 (A) and 16 (B). The absolute values depend on the scaling (Lever et al. 2017), but the ordering, with independent activity far higher-dimensional than the real data and B, does not.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Caveats:* PCA finds directions of maximal variance, which are not necessarily the most informative directions. z-scoring gives noisy, quiet cells as much weight as active ones. The participation ratio depends on the bin size and the number of time points."
   ]
