   "source": [
    "**Method: classical MDS (principal coordinates analysis) on cosine dissimilarity**, as in the lectures (Lecture 4-2; `demo_w6.py`; Mitchell-Heggs et al. 2022).\n",
    "1. The dissimilarity between two population vectors is $d_{ij} = 1 - \\cos\\theta_{ij} = 1 - \\frac{\\mathbf r_i\\cdot\\mathbf r_j}{\\lVert\\mathbf r_i\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\mathbf r_j\\rVert}$. It compares *which* cells are active, independent of the overall magnitude, and unlike PCA it does not centre the data.\n",
    "2. Double-centre: $B = -\\tfrac12 H D^{(2)} H$ with $H = I - \\tfrac1n\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^\\top$. Eigendecompose $B$ and use $\\sqrt{\\lambda_k}\\,\\mathbf v_k$ as coordinates (the lecture's `cmdscale`).\n",
    "\n",
    "*Why this method:* cosine dissimilarity is insensitive to the gain fluctuations that dominated PC1 in 4a, so it should show stimulus structure more directly. *Assumptions:* PCA assumes the important structure lies along directions of high variance in a linear, centred, Euclidean space. Classical MDS assumes the dissimilarities can be represented as Euclidean distances, and with Euclidean distances it is exactly PCA (lecture). With cosine dissimilarities that assumption is only approximate, and **negative eigenvalues** of $B$ measure the part of the geometry that no Euclidean map can represent.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is applied to (i) the 598 grating responses and (ii) the real movie population vectors in 0.5-s bins over all 10 repeats (600 points; the *same* input is given to PCA for comparison). To compare the 3-D maps quantitatively we use cross-validated 10-nearest-neighbour decoding of direction and TF (gratings), and, for the movie, the fraction of points whose nearest neighbour in another repeat is the same movie moment (±1 s)."
   ]
