    "**Discussion 4c.**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Gratings.* In the cosine-MDS map the presentations form clusters by direction, and **opposite directions lie next to each other** (for example 0° and 180° in the upper left). This is the signature of orientation tuning: cells that respond to a grating orientation respond to both of its drift directions. The covariance PCA (left) shows the same stimuli differently: a star whose arms are single directions carried by a few strongly tuned cells, with most presentations piled up near the origin.\n",
    "\n",
    "Decoding direction from 3 dimensions with 10 nearest neighbours: cosine MDS 0.50, covariance PCA 0.50, z-scored PCA 0.37 (chance 0.125; all 296 z-scored cells 0.48). **MDS and PCA of the same unscaled responses perform equally well.** The real difference is the **normalisation of the input**: z-scoring each cell gives weakly responsive, noisy cells the same weight as strongly tuned ones and buries the direction signal (Lever et al. 2017). MDS being non-linear does not explain the difference. Temporal frequency is poorly decoded by every 3-D map (0.24–0.27, chance 0.20), consistent with 4a.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Movie.* Both maps show a progression through movie time. Nearest-neighbour matching across repeats is slightly better for MDS than PCA (0.46 against 0.42, chance about 0.07), but both lose a lot compared with the full cosine space (0.77). Three dimensions do not hold the movie representation.\n",
    "\n",
    "*How Euclidean is the cosine geometry?* 28% (gratings) and 20% (movie) of the eigenvalue magnitude is negative, so a 3-D Euclidean map distorts it substantially. In the Shepard diagram, most pairs have dissimilarity near 1 (sparse response vectors are nearly orthogonal) and their embedded distances are compressed into a broad band.\n",
    "\n",
    "*What each method reveals or obscures.* **PCA** is linear, has interpretable loadings (which cells make up each axis), and can project new data onto the axes. But it ranks directions by variance and is not scale invariant: depending on the scaling it is dominated either by a handful of high-amplitude cells (covariance) or by the shared gain fluctuation (z-scored, 4a). **Cosine MDS** preserves the similarity of activity *patterns*. Because each population vector is normalised to unit length, it ignores the overall gain on each presentation (while still weighting cells by their ΔF/F), which makes the orientation structure visible. But its axes have no loadings, its sign and rotation are arbitrary, it needs an n × n matrix (impractical for long recordings without binning), new points cannot easily be added, and the negative eigenvalues show that the map is necessarily approximate. Both are global methods: a curved manifold would need a local method such as Isomap or UMAP."
   ]
