    "**Synthesis.**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Stimulus-locked but only partially reliable.** The movie drives a minority of cells reliably (13% with r > 0.3). The population state recurs at 30-s lags at about four times the background rate, and delay reconstructions of the best neuron return to the same movie moment about six times more often than chance. Surrogate B reproduces this qualitatively but greatly overestimates its strength (median reliability 0.55 against 0.12; RR at 30 s 0.78 against 0.17), because it drives every neuron identically on every repeat.\n",
    "2. **No evidence for low-dimensional autonomous deterministic dynamics.** The false-neighbour fraction never reaches zero, the best neuron's embedding cannot be told apart from linear (IAAFT) surrogates with the same spectrum, the population signal's departure from them coincides with non-stationarity, DET does not robustly distinguish the data from a noise surrogate, and much of the apparent smoothness comes from the calcium indicator. The structure that exists is driven by the input (a non-autonomous system), which is outside the assumptions of Takens' theorem.\n",
    "3. **Strong shared population fluctuations.** The real data's largest PC is the population mean (gain), pairwise correlations (0.073) far exceed those of either surrogate, and the population is much lower-dimensional than independent activity. These fluctuations are partly linked to running, which cuts the repeat-to-repeat recurrence about six-fold. Neither surrogate has shared variability beyond B's running gain.\n",
    "4. **Behaviour is not a simple Markov chain.** Running bouts are approximately memoryless, but stationary periods mix brief pauses with long rests and the hazard of starting to run decreases the longer the mouse has rested.\n",
    "5. **Geometry of stimulus coding.** Grating responses are organised by direction and orientation. That organisation appears in pattern (cosine) space and in covariance PCA, where a few strongly tuned cells carry it, but is masked by the gain and baseline axis once every cell is z-scored. How the data are scaled matters as much as the choice of method.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What the surrogates capture:** A captures the indicator time scale, noise and amplitude, i.e. everything that does not depend on stimulus or behaviour. B adds stimulus locking and gain modulation by state. **What a better model would need:** a shared latent gain or common input; heterogeneous stimulus drive (only some cells reliably driven); sparse, bursty firing with refractoriness; running gain applied at the *real* running times and with a semi-Markov behavioural model; and a non-linear (saturating) calcium model."
